## Ironclad engineering standards

### Language

All project content and output — code, comments, commit messages,
identifiers, documentation, and chat responses when producing project
artifacts — is written in English, regardless of what language the user
writes their prompts in. This keeps code searchable and diffs reviewable
across contributors who do not share a first language.

### AI usage policy (condensed)

- The AI assistant supports engineering decisions, it does not replace
  engineering ownership. The developer who accepts, modifies, merges, or
  deploys code remains responsible for it, whether or not AI generated
  it. AI-generated code gets the same review, tests, and standards as any
  other code.
- Never paste real personal data (student/guardian data, or any other
  personal data this project handles) into a chat session
  to debug an issue; use synthetic or anonymized data instead.
- Confirmation protocol: a short plan and explicit go-ahead is required
  before coding for database schema changes, new dependencies,
  architectural decisions, removing or significantly restructuring
  existing code, major version upgrades of a language/runtime/framework,
  or any genuinely ambiguous requirement. Not required for bug fixes with
  a clear reproduction, a missing test, small isolated functions, or
  formatting.
- State low-stakes assumptions in one line instead of silently picking
  one or stopping to ask.
- If the same approach fails twice in a row, stop and report instead of
  trying further variations unprompted.
- Touch only what the task requires; flag unrelated cleanup as a
  suggestion instead of doing it inline. Do not generate unrequested
  README files, docstrings, or comments.
- Prefer the most recent stable or long-term-support version of a
  language, runtime, or framework that still receives security patches.
  Dependencies stay pinned in a lockfile; updates are deliberate.
- Do not reference or call a symbol (function, method, flag, environment
  variable, path, or library API) you have not confirmed exists in the
  code or current documentation. Verify, do not recall from memory. When
  stating a fact about the code, cite the file and line. Say you need to
  check rather than presenting a guess as fact.
- Read only the part of a file you need, do not re-read what is already
  in context, and show diffs rather than reprinting unchanged files.
  Cheap on output, never cheap on verification.

### Intensity

Ironclad runs at one of three intensity levels: `core`, `full`, or
`max`. The level controls how much of the adjustable guidance applies,
it never drops the floor. `language.md`, `ai-usage-policy.md`, and
`security.md` (plus `lgpd.md` when the project handles personal data)
apply at every level. The level is read from an `.ironclad` file at the
project root (its first non-empty, non-comment line names the level), and
defaults to `full` when that file is absent. At `core`, consult only the
single most task-specific file for a task; at `full`, every file the task
maps to; at `max`, also apply `review-checklist.md` and treat the
coverage floor as mandatory. See `{{CONTENT_DIR}}/intensity.md`.

